TERMIC AMIGA WORKSPACE

Native MUI development workspace for AmigaOS 3.x: projects and independent
source-copy tasks, embedded source editing, cloud-agent conversations,
local builds, program execution, AmigaDOS commands and local Git review.
The application and compiler run on the Amiga. No Mac, Docker, DevBench or
LAN model server is needed at runtime.

REQUIREMENTS
68020+, AmigaOS 3.2, MUI 3.8+, TextEditor.mcc 15.56, Fast RAM, VBCC and NDK.
Cloud access needs Roadshow or compatible bsdsocket.library, AmiSSL with CA
certificates, an accurate clock and an OpenAI API key. The bundled native
Git client was verified with AmiSSL 5.27; install that runtime from its
upstream distribution. The older 5.23 developer headers remain compatible.
Git repositories require long filenames (PFS3/SFS or FFS with long names).

INSTALL
Unpack into a new drawer. In an Amiga Shell:
  Assign Termic: Work:TermicNative
  Execute Termic:InstallSDK
  Execute Termic:InstallUI
  Execute Termic:BuildNative
  Termic:TermicNative --icon Termic:TermicNative
Replace Work:TermicNative with your chosen drawer, then double-click the
TermicNative icon. The application assigns Termic: to its own drawer.
InstallUI adds TextEditor only if absent; it never replaces your MUI core.
SDK archives include their original licences. Startup files are not edited.
The AmiSSL 5.27 runtime archive is in SDK/amissl527.lha; extract it into a
temporary drawer and run its original Install-AmiSSL installer if needed.

On an FFS volume with 30-character filenames, optionally create independent
64 MiB project storage after InstallUI and BuildNative:
  Termic:TermicStorage --create
This creates a new Projects.hdf file and initializes only that new image.
It refuses an existing image or a disk-image unit owned by another device.
Settings then uses TMGIT:Projects; Termic mounts that image automatically.
No existing partition is formatted and no startup file is changed. Unit 7
of diskimage.device is reserved for this optional volume. Use another
long-filename drawer in Settings if the unit is needed by another app.
To back up the image, close active jobs and use a Shell outside TMGIT:
  Termic:TermicStorage --eject
Copy Projects.hdf, then run Termic:TermicStorage to mount it again.

WORKSPACE
Left: projects, project naming/import, tasks and task status.
Centre: Agent, Editor, Build / Shell, Changes and Settings tabs.
Right: editable C, header, text and Markdown files, plus new-file controls.
Drag the dividers to resize panes. Disk fonts improve readability when
Helvetica and Courier are available; otherwise MUI fonts are used.

New project uses the clean Template drawer. Import source drawer copies
regular editable files into a managed project. New task copies the selected
workspace's source and build command into its own Tasks/task-NNNN drawer.
It does not copy Git internals, credentials or conversation IDs. These are
source snapshots, not Git worktrees. The installed amiga-git has no worktree
command. Original Project/main.c remains available as the starter project.
Names and task status are saved, and the last workspace reopens on launch.

EDITOR
Select a file to edit it in TextEditor.mcc. Save, Undo, Redo, Cut, Copy,
Paste, Find and Next operate in the application. A successful save keeps
previous contents in file.bak. File/workspace switches save pending edits;
a failed save prevents switching. Source files are limited to 32 KiB.
Task instructions are edited in the Agent tab, not in a second editor copy.

BUILD AND RUN
Build uses the current workspace, produces Program, and displays diagnostics
in Build / Shell. The default compiles main.c with VBCC. Settings can save
a different build command per workspace, including additional C files and
libraries. It must produce Program.new; BuildProject promotes it on success.
A failed build retains the last executable, so Run would launch that older build.
MUI headers and mui_compat.h are on the installed include search path.
Run launches the compiled program. Only an explicit Run or shell command
runs generated executables. The shell field executes AmigaDOS commands in
the selected workspace and displays their output here; it is not a PTY.

Different tasks can run separate workers, up to 16 per application. One
worker at a time owns each task; its editor is read-only during that job.
The UI sleeps when idle and refreshes output only while jobs are active.
Cancel signals the worker. Network waits observe cancellation; arbitrary
child programs may require their own Quit action before the worker returns.

GIT
Changes offers Init repository, Status, Diff, and Stage source and commit.
Set repository identity in the Shell tab before the first commit:
  Termic:Tools/amiga-git/git config user.name "Your Name"
  Termic:Tools/amiga-git/git config user.email you@example.com
The commit action stages existing C/header files. Use the shell for deleted
files, branches, remotes, fetch/push, merge, stash and other Git operations.
Commit messages accept letters, digits, spaces and . , : / ( ) _ - .
New repositories ignore build products and Termic job metadata. Existing
.gitignore files are retained. No remote operation runs automatically.

AGENT
Enter task instructions, select a model and save an API key in Settings.
The secret field never loads the existing key. Leaving it blank preserves
that key. Start agent sends the task and requested source/compiler output
to OpenAI. The model may list/read/write C/header files and build. It has
no shell-command tool, and file tools reject device paths and traversal.
Completed conversation IDs persist per task; New conversation resets the
ID while retaining the transcript. API responses are stored for continuity.
Each run makes at most eight requests. API access and billing are required.
The default model is gpt-5-mini; choose a model available to your account.

The local Config/api-key file is plaintext on classic AmigaOS. It is sent
only as the HTTPS Authorization header to api.openai.com, never in command
arguments, prompts, logs or installation packages. TLS verifies certificates
and hostnames and requires TLS 1.2+. Test verified HTTPS expects HTTP 401
without a key; it does not establish authenticated model access.

SHORTCUTS
F1 Agent; F2 Editor; F3 Build / Shell; F4 Changes; F5 Settings.
F6 Build; F7 Run; F8 Save; F9 New task; F10 Quit.
Shift+F1 starts the agent; Shift+F2 focuses the new filename (Enter creates).
Shift+F3 focuses the shell command; Shift+F4 focuses the commit message.
Enter executes the shell command or commits from those fields.
Shift+F5 initializes Git; Shift+F6 shows status; Shift+F7 shows the diff;
Shift+F8 marks done; Shift+F9 creates a project; Shift+F10 cancels a job.
Text editing and clipboard keys follow TextEditor.mcc preferences.

BOUNDARIES
This native implementation does not run the React/Tauri frontend or desktop
agent CLIs. There is no xterm terminal, streamed token display, automatic
queue, broadcast/race mode, integrated remote PR UI or Git worktree support.
The shell exposes the installed native Git client's additional commands.
Task instructions: 8 KiB. Individual source: 32 KiB. Agent compiler output:
16 KiB. HTTP response: 256 KiB. File list: 128 files, six directory levels.
Network deadline: 120 seconds after DNS, whose timeout is stack-controlled.
Long imports enumerate/copy synchronously; large trees can delay the UI.

Classic AmigaOS provides no process sandbox: compiler jobs and generated
programs retain normal OS access. Review changes before Run. After a worker
crash, inspect its job.log and remove its .busy drawer only when the worker
has stopped. One Termic installation should own the Termic: assign at a time.

ZZ9000
The existing Workbench RTG display is used. Compilation, TLS and file work
run on the 68k. No ARM payload, firmware or display-mode change is made.
ARM acceleration needs a measured workload, exclusive core ownership and
the verified shared-memory/cache contract from the hardware workspace.

VERIFICATION
Host: python3 scripts/amiga/test_native.py (synthetic API, ASan/UBSan).
Hardware: scripts/amiga/accept_mui.py through the existing DevBench MCP.
The hardware suite uses isolated fixtures and never submits a model request
or reads credentials. See docs/amiga-development.md for verified scope.

